263 bytes).\\nTranscript written on paper.log.\\nLatexmk: Getting log file \'paper.log\'\\nLatexmk: Examining \'paper.fls\'\\nLatexmk: Examining \'paper.log\'\\nLatexmk: Found input bbl file \'paper.bbl\'\\nLatexmk: Log file says output to \'paper.pdf\'\\nLatexmk: Found bibliography file(s):\\n  ./references.bib\\nLatexmk: All targets () are up-to-date\\n\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \\"rg -n \'Warning|undefined|Overfull|Underfull|Error\' paper/paper.log paper/paper.blg; cat paper/paper.bbl | tail -70\\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \\"rg -n \'Warning|undefined|Overfull|Underfull|Error\' paper/paper.log paper/paper.blg; cat paper/paper.bbl | tail -70\\"","aggregated_output":"\\\\begin{thebibliography}{1}\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{bergemann2026mechanism}\\nDirk Bergemann, Andrew Koh, and Stephen Morris.\\n\\\\newblock Mechanism design for alignment and control, 2026.\\n\\\\newblock URL: \\\\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01595}, \\\\href\\n  {https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01595} {\\\\path{arXiv:2609.01595}}.\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{li2025combining}\\nZun Li, Marc Lanctot, Kevin~R. McKee, Luke Marris, Ian Gemp, Daniel Hennes,\\n  Paul Muller, Kate Larson, Yoram Bachrach, and Michael~P. Wellman.\\n\\\\newblock Combining deep reinforcement learning and search with generative\\n  models for game-theoretic opponent modeling.\\n\\\\newblock In {\\\\em Proceedings of the Thirty-Fourth International Joint\\n  Conference on Artificial Intelligence}, pages 161--169, 2025.\\n\\\\newblock URL: \\\\url{https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2025/19}, \\\\href\\n  {https://doi.org/10.24963/ijcai.2025/19} {\\\\path{doi:10.24963/ijcai.2025/19}}.\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{wyse2026commitment}\\nTim Wyse, Kaitlin Bustos, Yulia Volkova, and Max Kleiman-Weiner.\\n\\\\newblock Commitment to cooperation with self-negotiated contracts, 2026.\\n\\\\newblock URL: \\\\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.22750}, \\\\href\\n  {https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.22750} {\\\\path{arXiv:2607.22750}}.\\n\\n\\\\end{thebibliography}\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \\"pdfinfo paper/paper.pdf | rg \'Pages:|Page size:|File size:\'; pdftotext -layout paper/paper.pdf - | head -100\\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \\"pdfinfo paper/paper.pdf | rg \'Pages:|Page size:|File size:\'; pdftotext -layout paper/paper.pdf - | head -100\\"","aggregated_output":"Pages:           4\\nPage size:       595.276 x 841.89 pts (A4)\\nFile size:       302263 bytes\\n  PATHFINDER PAPER · Q4P9          ·   2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n  A Reciprocal Contract Witness for\\n  Asymmetric CT-Bench Boards\\n  Research phase: DRAFT after 1 round, 0 ITERATE, 0 REVISE. Edit phase: paper, review round 1\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  Abstract\\n\\n  We give a finite, computationally checked contract-and-path witness for the asymmetric board class\\n  of CT-Bench. For each of its 396 boards, a five-clause programmatic tile contract and a shared\\n  shortest route make both players strictly exceed their non-interaction scores while attaining the game’s\\n  maximum joint score. An accounting identity states the delivered net coverage Red needs to improve\\n  on its outside option. These are feasibility statements. They do not establish that agents will propose\\n  or accept the contract, or that its path is an equilibrium of the sequential game.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n1 Question and setting\\n  Bergemann, Koh and Morris distinguish feasible action rules from mechanisms that induce\\n  truthful reporting and obedience. Their multi-agent formulation concerns a one-shot action\\n  after reports; it does not supply an equilibrium theorem for the sequential contracting game\\n  studied here [1]. Wyse and colleagues introduce CT-Bench and measure how language-model\\n  agents negotiate and use contracts in that game [3]. The distinction motivates a narrower\\n  question: can P’s asymmetric boards support a strictly mutually beneficial outcome through\\n  P’s actual tile-contract interface?\\n     In P’s 4 × 4 boards, both players start at one corner and seek the opposite green goal. The\\n  other 14 cells comprise seven Red and seven Blue cells; Red starts with 14 Red and two\\n  green chips, and Blue with 14 Blue and two green chips. Moving onto a cell consumes a chip\\n  of its colour. A finisher scores 20 plus 5 per remaining chip, while a non-finisher scores zero.\\n  In the asymmetric class Red has an own-chip route and Blue has none. Their no-interaction\\n  scores are ( 𝑏𝑅 , 𝑏 𝐵 ) = (70, 0), and P gives 140 as the maximum joint score. P samples 40\\n  boards from this class for its agent evaluation [3].\\n     P’s programmatic tile contract (PTC) specifies a tile, giver, receiver and colour in each\\n  clause. Once the players accept the compiled contract, an attempted covered move triggers\\n  a transfer automatically if the giver has the chip; the giver scores zero if it cannot provide\\n  one. The construction below uses only this stated format and enough initial chips to fulfil\\n  every clause [3].\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                                            1\\n\\f  Pathfinder paper                                                               Q4P9   · 2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n2 Finite construction\\n  The recorded exhaustive check enumerates all 14\\n                                                     \\u0001\\n                                                   7 = 3,432 balanced boards. Exactly 396\\n  have independent Red and dependent Blue. A shortest Blue path has six moves: five onto\\n  coloured cells and one onto the green goal. Among shortest Blue paths, the minimum number\\n  𝑘 of Red cells is one on 314 boards and two on 82. A separate enumeration of all simple\\n  paths found no longer path with fewer Red cells. These counts come from the peer oracle,\\n  independently checked in the ledger; they are computational findings rather than a newly\\n  derived closed-form count.\\n\\n  Proposition 1 (Reciprocal tile-contract witness). For each of the 396 asymmetric boards under\\n  P’s stated generation rule, there is a PTC with five coloured-tile clauses and a pair of six-move\\n  paths whose executed scores are (85, 55) or (75, 65). Both players strictly exceed (70, 0), and\\n  their joint score is 140.\\n\\n  Proof. Choose a shortest Blue path minimising its Red-cell count 𝑘 ∈ {1, 2}. Have both\\n  players traverse that same path. For each of its 𝑘 Red cells, the contract makes Red cover\\n  Blue’s move; for each of its 5 − 𝑘 Blue cells, it makes Blue cover Red’s move. Each player\\n  pays for its own matching-colour moves and its own green goal move. Red therefore spends\\n  2𝑘 + 1 chips and Blue spends 2(5 − 𝑘) + 1. Neither inventory is exhausted. As both begin\\n  with 16 chips, their scores are\\n                                                                       \\u0001\\n     ( 𝑟𝑅 , 𝑟 𝐵 ) = 20 + 5[16 − (2𝑘 + 1)] , 20 + 5[16 − (2(5 − 𝑘) + 1)] = (95 − 10𝑘, 45 + 10𝑘) .\\n\\n  The checked values of 𝑘 yield the stated pairs. Both paths have the minimum six moves, so\\n  the sum is P’s joint maximum of 140.                                                    □\\n\\n      This is an existence result conditional on the contract being accepted and its specified\\n  route being followed. Automatic transfer removes an execution choice at each contracted\\n  tile when inventory is sufficient. It does not establish incentives to negotiate these clauses or\\n  full sequential obedience after acceptance.\\n\\n\\n\\n3 Delivered coverage and observed outcomes\\n  Proposition 2 (Red’s score identity). Suppose Red finishes in 𝐿𝑅 ≥ 6 moves, Blue pays for 𝑐 of\\n  Red’s moves, and Red pays for 𝑑 of Blue’s. With no other chip trades or point transfers,\\n                                              \\u0002                \\u0003\\n                                  𝑟𝑅 = 70 + 5 𝑐 − 𝑑 − ( 𝐿𝑅 − 6) .\\n\\n  Thus Red strictly beats its own outside score precisely when 𝑐 − 𝑑 > 𝐿𝑅 − 6.\\n\\n  Proof. Red’s remaining inventory is 16 − 𝐿𝑅 + 𝑐 − 𝑑 . Applying P’s terminal score rule gives\\n  20 + 5(16 − 𝐿𝑅 + 𝑐 − 𝑑 ), which rearranges to the identity. The proposition concerns Red’s\\n  score; both-beat-baseline additionally requires Blue to finish with a positive score. For the\\n  reciprocal witness, 𝑐 = 5 − 𝑘, 𝑑 = 𝑘 and 𝐿𝑅 = 6, so the inequality holds for both recorded\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftoppm -f 1 -l 1 -scale-to 1200 -png -singlefile paper/paper.pdf /tmp/q4p9-paper-page1 && ls -lh /tmp/q4p9-paper-page1.png\'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftoppm -f 1 -l 1 -scale-to 1200 -png -singlefile paper/paper.pdf /tmp/q4p9-paper-page1 && ls -lh /tmp/q4p9-paper-page1.png\'","aggregated_output":"-rw-r--r--@ 1 v  wheel   171K Sep 25 22:52 /tmp/q4p9-paper-page1.png\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \\"cat /Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/engine/pathfinder/styles/pathfinder-paper.sty | head -160; sed -n \'1,200p\' /Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/engine/pathfinder/styles/pathfinder-common.sty\\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","c