:           4\\nFile size:       302540 bytes\\n  PATHFINDER PAPER · Q4P9          ·   2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n  A Reciprocal Contract Witness for\\n  Asymmetric CT-Bench Boards\\n  Q   Mechanism Design for Alignment and Control arXiv:2609.01595\\n  P   Commitment To Cooperation With Self-Negotiated Contracts arXiv:2607.22750\\n\\n  Research phase: DRAFT after 1 round, 0 ITERATE, 0 REVISE. Edit phase: paper, review round 1\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  Abstract\\n\\n  We give a finite, computationally checked contract-and-path witness for the asymmetric board class\\n  of CT-Bench. For each of its 396 boards, a five-clause programmatic tile contract and a shared\\n  shortest route make both players strictly exceed their non-interaction scores while attaining the game’s\\n  maximum joint score. An accounting identity states the delivered net coverage Red needs to improve\\n  on its outside option. These are feasibility statements. They do not establish that agents will propose\\n  or accept the contract, or that its path is an equilibrium of the sequential game.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n1 Question and setting\\n  Bergemann, Koh and Morris distinguish feasible action rules from mechanisms that induce\\n  truthful reporting and obedience. Their multi-agent formulation concerns a one-shot action\\n  after reports; it does not supply an equilibrium theorem for the sequential contracting game\\n  studied here [1]. Wyse and colleagues introduce CT-Bench and measure how language-model\\n  agents negotiate and use contracts in that game [3]. The distinction motivates a narrower\\n  question: can P’s asymmetric boards support a strictly mutually beneficial outcome through\\n  P’s actual tile-contract interface?\\n     In P’s 4 × 4 boards, both players start at one corner and seek the opposite green goal. The\\n  other 14 cells comprise seven Red and seven Blue cells; Red starts with 14 Red and two\\n  green chips, and Blue with 14 Blue and two green chips. Moving onto a cell consumes a chip\\n  of its colour. A finisher scores 20 plus 5 per remaining chip, while a non-finisher scores zero.\\n  In the asymmetric class Red has an own-chip route and Blue has none. Their no-interaction\\n  scores are ( 𝑏𝑅 , 𝑏 𝐵 ) = (70, 0), and P gives 140 as the maximum joint score. P samples 40\\n11:Mechanism Design for Alignment and Control arXiv:2609.01595\\n15:Commitment To Cooperation With Self-Negotiated Contracts arXiv:2607.22750\\n154:References\\n156:and control, 2026. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01595, arXiv:2609.01595.\\n174:2750, arXiv:2607.22750.\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \\"python3 /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py paper/paper.pdf; rg -n \'LaTeX Warning|Package .*Warning|Overfull|Underfull|undefined|Error\' paper/paper.log paper/paper.blg || true\\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \\"python3 /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py paper/paper.pdf; rg -n \'LaTeX Warning|Package .*Warning|Overfull|Underfull|undefined|Error\' paper/paper.log paper/paper.blg || true\\"","aggregated_output":"style-gate: checked 1 file(s), 0 error(s), 0 warning(s)\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftoppm -f 1 -l 1 -scale-to 1200 -png -singlefile paper/paper.pdf /tmp/q4p9-paper-page1-final\'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftoppm -f 4 -l 4 -scale-to 1000 -png -singlefile paper/paper.pdf /tmp/q4p9-paper-page4-final\'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","com