ining \'note.fls\'\\nLatexmk: Examining \'note.log\'\\nLatexmk: Found input bbl file \'note.bbl\'\\nLatexmk: Log file says output to \'note.pdf\'\\nLatexmk: Found bibliography file(s):\\n  ./references.bib\\nLatexmk: All targets () are up-to-date\\n\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftotext -layout note.pdf - | tail -70 && cat note.bbl\'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftotext -layout note.pdf - | tail -70 && cat note.bbl\'","aggregated_output":"\\n\\n4 Objections and unresolved points\\n  The fill probability uses private seller costs and an assumed policy for every possible next\\n  trader. The auction buyer sees its own value, the order book and histories, but not those costs.\\n  The peers therefore treated 7/22 as an analyst’s counterfactual probability. An implementable\\n  test would need either an explicit oracle or a belief estimated from information available to\\n  the trader. No supplied result shows that the constructed late state appeared in the auction\\n  runs, or that these traders optimised CVaR. Indeed, the expected one-sample choice here is\\n  also the expected-profit choice.\\n     The peers also raised two distinct market objections. A buyer who crosses now can take a\\n  seller whom a higher-value buyer might otherwise have reached; more crossing therefore\\n  need not raise total surplus. Separately, the risk paper shows that adding individual CVaRs\\n  generally differs from taking CVaR of aggregate loss. The auction paper reports means\\n  across ten independent runs, not the downside distribution of efficiency. For example, ten\\n  efficiencies all equal to 0.91 and a set with nine values of 1 and one of 0.1 have the same\\n  mean but very different worst runs. That illustration does not claim to reproduce the auction’s\\n  underlying data.\\n     The quadratic taxes, equilibrium assumptions and value-error bounds of the risk paper\\n  do not carry over to this discrete auction decision. The auction’s ten evaluation runs are also\\n  different from the number of observations in a trader’s CVaR batch. The ledger’s outside\\n  sources already cover risk-based continuous double-auction bidding [5], CVaR bidding in a\\n  double-auctioned power market [1], and sampling bias in CVaR [4]. The recorded searches\\n  do not establish priority for the proposed combination.\\n\\n\\n\\n5 Why the thread stopped\\n  The verifier accepted the mathematical ranking reversal but paused the thread because it\\n  rests on a stipulated continuation and a fill probability unavailable to the auction’s traders.\\n  The record contains no evidence that relevant states occur or change market outcomes. The\\n  smallest restart is to count reachable late states in the auction simulator and, at matched states\\n  with fixed other-trader policies, estimate the action-specific continuation distributions. That\\n  first check would show whether the reversal appears at all; only then could an information-\\n  respecting belief and market-wide outcome test support a claim about these agents or their\\n  markets.\\n\\n\\n\\n  References\\n  [1] Panda et al. Cvar bidding in a double-auctioned power market, 2019. URL: https:\\n      //doi.org/10.1049/iet-gtd.2018.5905.\\n\\n  [2] Pathfinder research campaign. Fixed-batch cvar and participation in a quadratic resource-\\n      allocation mechanism, September 2026. Local manuscript, pathfinder-local-S4.\\n\\n  [3] Pawel Struski, Jakub Swistak, Inez Okulska, and Przemyslaw Biecek. Competitive\\n      market behavior of llms, September 2026. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02580,\\n      arXiv:2609.02580.\\n\\n  [4] Tamar et al. Sampling bias in cvar optimisation, 2015. URL: https://doi.org/10.1609/\\n      aaai.v29i1.9561.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                                  3\\n\\fPathfinder readable note                                             Q3P4 · 2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n[5] Vytelingum et al. Risk-based continuous double-auction bidding, 2004. URL: https:\\n    //eprints.soton.ac.uk/259567/.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                         4\\n\\f\\\\begin{thebibliography}{1}\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{Panda2019}\\nPanda et~al.\\n\\\\newblock Cvar bidding in a double-auctioned power market, 2019.\\n\\\\newblock URL: \\\\url{https://doi.org/10.1049/iet-gtd.2018.5905}.\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{P}\\n{Pathfinder research campaign}.\\n\\\\newblock Fixed-batch cvar and participation in a quadratic resource-allocation\\n  mechanism, September 2026.\\n\\\\newblock Local manuscript, pathfinder-local-S4.\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{Q}\\nPawel Struski, Jakub Swistak, Inez Okulska, and Przemyslaw Biecek.\\n\\\\newblock Competitive market behavior of llms, September 2026.\\n\\\\newblock URL: \\\\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02580}, \\\\href\\n  {https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02580} {\\\\path{arXiv:2609.02580}}.\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{Tamar2015}\\nTamar et~al.\\n\\\\newblock Sampling bias in cvar optimisation, 2015.\\n\\\\newblock URL: \\\\url{https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v29i1.9561}.\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{Vytelingum2004}\\nVytelingum et~al.\\n\\\\newblock Risk-based continuous double-auction bidding, 2004.\\n\\\\newblock URL: \\\\url{https://eprints.soton.ac.uk/259567/}.\\n\\n\\\\end{thebibliography}\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \\"pdfinfo note.pdf | rg \'Pages:|Page size:|File size:\' && pdftotext -layout note.pdf - | head -65 && rg -n \'Warning:|Overfull|Underfull|undefined|Citation\' note.log | tail -30\\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \\"pdfinfo note.pdf | rg \'Pages:|Page size:|File size:\' && pdftotext -layout note.pdf - | head -65 && rg -n \'Warning:|Overfull|Underfull|undefined|Citation\' note.log | tail -30\\"","aggregated_output":"Pages:           4\\nPage size:       595.276 x 841.89 pts (A4)\\nFile size:       289724 bytes\\n  PATHFINDER READABLE NOTE · Q3P4            · 2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n  Tail Risk at the Last Quote: A Conditional\\n  Link Between Two Market Papers\\n  Research phase: PAUSE after 1 round, 0 ITERATE, 0 REVISE. Edit phase: readable note\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  Abstract\\n\\n  Struski and colleagues study language-model traders in a double auction, while the companion local\\n  manuscript studies fixed-batch estimates of conditional value at risk in a different allocation mechanism.\\n  We asked whether the sampling issue in the second paper could change a late trader’s choice between\\n  taking a sure profit and waiting for a better one. The peers found a valid reversal for a constructed\\n  auction state: an expected one-sample risk estimate favours waiting, while true tail risk favours trading\\n  now. The continuation behaviour and the fill probability were assumed, and no such state was shown\\n  to occur in the reported runs. The thread therefore paused pending a matched-state check in the\\n  auction simulator.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n1 Introduction\\n  Competitive Market Behavior of LLMs puts language-model buyers and sellers in a double\\n  auction [3]. A buyer may accept the best current selling price, or post a better buying offer\\n  and hope someone accepts it later. Each round has at most 300 chances to act, so a better\\n  offer can expire without a trade. The agents are told to maximise profit. The paper reports\\n  that one model often makes tiny price improvements and leaves profitable trades unfinished;\\n  its markets reach lower efficiency than the best-performing model’s markets.\\n      Fixed-Batch CVaR and Participation in a Quadratic Resource-Allocation Mechanism studies\\n  a separate allocation game [2]. It measures risk with conditional value at risk, or CVaR:\\n  the average loss in the worst chosen fraction of outcomes. Its main example shows that\\n  optimising a fixed-batch estimate, even in expectation over fresh batches, can lead to a\\n  choice that fares worse under true risk. Its equilibrium and welfare bounds require the\\n  assumptions of its quadratic mechanism. The two papers called “Q” and “P” inside that\\n  manuscript are other works, not the pair considered here.\\n      The connection pursued was a late auction choice. Accepting a standing offer secures a\\n  modest gain; improving one’s own offer may yield a larger gain, but may never be filled. The\\n  peers asked whether fixed-batch CVaR could rank those actions differently from true CVaR.\\n  This was a counterfactual question: the auction paper gives its traders no CVaR objective or\\n  sampling oracle.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                                            1\\n\\f  Pathfinder readable note                                                       Q3P4 · 2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n2 What was done\\n  The peers first read the auction rules and the risk paper’s distinction between true CVaR and\\n  the expected CVaR of an empirical batch. Ada also asked whether the auction paper’s means\\n  across ten runs could reveal the downside of market efficiency. They cannot: different sets\\n  of run outcomes can have the same mean and very different worst outcomes. This raised a\\n  separate question about market-wide risk.\\n     Emmy then reduced the trading question to two actions at the end of a round. Crossing\\n  the standing offer gives a sure profit. Posting a noncrossing offer gives a larger profit only\\n  if a later trader accepts it. Emmy derived the risk ranking for this choice and placed it\\n  in a state allowed by the auction rules. Ada checked the seller count, the resulting fill\\n  probability, and the surplus arithmetic against those rules. Their derivation and checks are\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftoppm -f 1 -l 1 -scale-to 1400 -png -singlefile note.pdf /tmp/q3p4-page1 && pdftoppm -f 4 -l 4 -scale-to 1400 -png -singlefile note.pdf /tmp/q3p4-page4\'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftoppm -f 1 -l 1 -scale-to 1400 -png -singlefile note.pdf /tmp/q3p4-page1 && pdftoppm -f 4 -l 4 -scale-to 1400 -png -singlefile note.pdf /tmp/q3p4-page4\'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftoppm -f 3 -l 3 -scale-to 1400 -png -singlefile note.pdf /tmp/q3p4-page3\'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \'pdftoppm -f 3 -l 3 -scale-to 1400 -png -singlefile note.pdf /tmp/q3p4-page3\'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"agent_message","text":"The first PDF build succeeds, and the references resolve. PDF inspection shows the last reference alone on page four, so I’m tightening the closing sections before the final build."}}', '{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"file_change","changes":[{"path":"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/reinjection/threads/Q3P4/edited/note.tex","kind":"update"},{"path":"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/reinjection/threads/Q3P4/edited/references.bib","kind":"update"}],"status":"in_progress"}}', '{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"file_change","changes":[{"path":"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/rein